1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
In 2010, getting a bank loan
meant paperwork and credit checks.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In 2024, a protocol lent
someone ten million dollars in seconds.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
No meeting. No signature.
No banker.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,500
The code processes the loan.
Instantly.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,000
This is DeFi.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
A smart contract is a program
that runs on a blockchain.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
When conditions are met,
it executes automatically.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
No one needs to approve it.
No one can stop it.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Think of a vending machine.
You insert money.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The machine checks the amount.
If correct, it releases the snack.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
No human involvement.
That's a smart contract.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Here's what it looks like in DeFi.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,500
IF you deposit 150 dollars of ETH
as collateral—

14
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,000
THEN the protocol releases
a 100-dollar stablecoin loan.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,500
IF your collateral drops
below 120 dollars—

16
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,000
THEN the protocol automatically
liquidates your position.

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The code checks these conditions
every few seconds.

18
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,500
No sleep. No lunch break.
No bias.

19
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,000
The code is the bank.

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
These protocols don't ask questions.
They execute rules.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Rules written in code.
Rules that anyone can read.

22
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Rules no one can change
without a governance vote.

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
That's what makes DeFi different
from traditional finance.

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,500
The mechanism is the rule.

25
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:37,500
No exceptions. No favors.
No closing hours.

26
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:42,000
A bank can deny your loan.
Delay your transfer.

27
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
A smart contract cannot.
It can only follow code.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And that code is public.
Anyone can verify it.

29
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,500
This is what decentralized finance
is actually built on.

30
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,500
Not trust in an institution.
Trust in mathematics.

31
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,500
Not trust in a person.
Trust in transparent code.

32
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,500
That's the foundation.
Everything else builds on this.

33
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,500
Let's look at what's been built.

34
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Starting with DeFi lending.

35
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Billions of dollars.
Lent by code. Without banks.

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Here's how it works.

37
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The two biggest DeFi lending protocols
are Aave and Compound.

38
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Combined, they've managed
billions in loans.

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Lenders deposit assets—
ETH, USDC, whatever they hold.

40
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
The protocol pays them interest
automatically.

41
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,500
No bank needed.

42
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,500
Borrowers come to the other side.

43
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:54,000
To borrow 100 dollars of USDC,
you post 150 dollars of ETH.

44
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
That's 150% collateralization.

45
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Why so high?
Because crypto prices move fast.

46
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,500
If ETH drops suddenly,
the protocol needs a cushion.

47
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:11,000
When collateral drops below threshold,
the protocol sells it automatically.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,500
Think of it like a pawn shop
with no pawnbroker.

49
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:19,500
You leave collateral. You get the loan.
If you don't pay—

50
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:23,000
the collateral is sold.

51
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Automatic. Instant. Unstoppable.

52
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,500
Interest rates aren't set by a committee.
They respond to supply and demand.

53
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,500
More borrowers — rates rise.
More lenders — rates fall.

54
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,500
All of this happens through code.
No human sets the rate.

55
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,000
And it runs 24 hours a day,
seven days a week.

56
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
No holidays. No system maintenance.
No business hours.

57
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
What you deposit earns yield.
What you borrow costs interest.

58
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,500
The protocol collects the spread.
Automatically.

59
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:01,000
No loan officer. No credit check.
No relationship required.

60
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,500
Just code, collateral, and a wallet.

61
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:10,000
Why would someone borrow
against assets they already own?

62
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,500
Tax efficiency. Access to liquidity.
Without selling your position.

63
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:19,000
That's why DeFi lending exists.
It fills a real need.

64
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Now: how do you trade
without anyone on the other side?

65
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
That's what an AMM solves.

66
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Traditional exchanges use order books.
Buyers and sellers list prices.

67
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,500
AMMs — Automated Market Makers —
work completely differently.

68
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,500
There's no order book.
Instead: a liquidity pool.

69
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:48,000
Two assets sit in a smart contract.
ETH and USDC, for example.

70
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
The price is set by a formula.

71
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,500
x times y equals k.
The constant product formula.

72
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:01,000
x is the ETH amount.
y is the USDC amount.

73
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,500
k is a constant.
It never changes.

74
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:10,000
Add ETH to the pool—
the ETH supply increases.

75
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,500
So ETH's relative price drops.
USDC becomes relatively scarce.

76
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:19,000
So USDC's price in the pool rises.

77
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
No one set that price.
The math set it.

78
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
No one sets the price.
The math does.

79
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,500
Protocols like Uniswap, Curve,
and Balancer use this model.

80
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:35,500
Anyone can provide liquidity.
Anyone can trade.

81
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,500
The protocol never closes.

82
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:44,000
To become a liquidity provider,
you deposit both assets.

83
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,500
In the same ratio as the pool.
Fifty-fifty by value.

84
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:53,000
You receive LP tokens in return.
These represent your share.

85
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,500
When you withdraw,
you get your share of the pool.

86
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:02,000
Plus the fees accumulated
while you were in.

87
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,500
Every trade pays a fee.
Usually 0.3%.

88
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:11,000
That fee goes to liquidity providers.
Proportionally to their share.

89
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,500
More volume in the pool—
more fees earned.

90
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:20,000
This is how passive yield
is generated in DeFi.

91
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,500
Without a financial intermediary.
Without a middleman.

92
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,000
Just a formula and two asset pools
doing their job.

93
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,500
The AMM never sleeps.
It processes trades continuously.

94
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:38,000
Now let's talk about the catch.

95
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,500
Because there is always a catch
when yield is involved.

96
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Where does the yield come from?
Two main sources.

97
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,500
Trading fees and protocol incentives.
Let's take them one at a time.

98
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,000
Trading fees: every swap pays 0.3%.
Those fees go to LP holders.

99
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,500
The more volume, the more you earn.
Volume is the key variable.

100
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:07,000
Protocol incentives are different.
They're funded by token issuance.

101
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,500
If the protocol is printing tokens
to pay you yield—

102
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:16,000
that yield has a cost.
The token loses value over time.

103
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Now: impermanent loss.

104
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,500
When you provide liquidity,
you deposit two assets.

105
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,000
If one asset's price changes significantly,
you end up with less total value.

106
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,500
Less total value than if you'd held
both assets in your wallet.

107
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:38,000
The loss is impermanent—
if prices return to the original ratio—

108
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,000
it disappears.
If they don't—

109
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
it becomes permanent.

110
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Another risk: smart contract bugs.

111
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,500
In 2020, the Harvest Finance protocol
lost 34 million dollars.

112
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,500
In a single transaction.
A price manipulation attack.

113
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:02,500
Smart contracts are code.
Code can have bugs.

114
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:07,000
Even audited code.
Even code that passed review.

115
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,500
Here's the hard truth about yield.

116
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:16,000
If the APY looks too high to be real—
it probably is.

117
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,500
If you don't understand
where the yield comes from—

118
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:24,500
that's your signal.
Walk away.

119
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:29,000
Sustainable yield has a source.
Unsustainable yield has a victim.

120
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,500
Remember this distinction.
It will protect you.

121
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:39,000
Now let's look at the asset
at the center of most DeFi activity.

122
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,000
The stablecoin.

123
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Stablecoins are crypto assets
designed to hold a fixed value.

124
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,500
Usually one dollar.
There are three main types.

125
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:58,000
Fiat-backed: USDC and USDT.
Real dollars held in reserve.

126
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,500
For every USDC token,
there's a dollar in a bank account.

127
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:07,000
Crypto-backed: DAI.
Backed by ETH and other crypto assets.

128
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,500
It maintains its peg
through overcollateralization.

129
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:15,500
And smart contract mechanisms.
Not a bank account.

130
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:20,000
Algorithmic: no reserves at all.
Supply adjustments and incentives.

131
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
The theory: the peg holds
because it's profitable to maintain it.

132
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
In May 2022, Terra's UST
was the third-largest stablecoin.

133
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,500
Algorithmic. No reserve backing.
Tens of billions in circulation.

134
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:39,000
Then the peg broke.

135
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,500
The mechanism that was supposed
to restore the peg—

136
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:47,500
made it worse.

137
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:52,000
UST fell from one dollar
to fractions of a cent.

138
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,500
Forty billion dollars in value
destroyed in four days.

139
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:00,500
The mechanism failed.
The peg broke.

140
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:05,500
Forty billion dollars.
Four days.

141
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:10,000
This is why the type of stablecoin matters.

142
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,500
Not all dollar tokens
carry the same risk.

143
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:19,000
The backing mechanism is the difference.
Understand it before you trust it.

144
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Now let's put all the risks
on the table at once.

145
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Because in DeFi,
they're all real.

146
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
DeFi has real risks.
Here they are.

147
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,500
Smart contract risk: the code can fail.
Even after multiple audits.

148
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:43,000
Protocol risk: the design can be flawed.
Governance can be captured.

149
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,500
Liquidation risk: if collateral falls fast,
you lose your entire position.

150
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:52,000
Rug pulls: developers build a protocol,
attract liquidity—

151
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and disappear with the funds.

152
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
This is fraud.

153
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,000
It happens.
And it's rarely recovered.

154
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,500
Oracle risk: protocols need
real-world price data.

155
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:12,500
If an oracle is manipulated,
the protocol can be drained.

156
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:17,000
This is not theoretical.
It has happened multiple times.

157
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:21,500
Regulatory risk: the legal landscape
around DeFi is still forming.

158
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:26,000
Protocols available today
may face restrictions tomorrow.

159
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,000
These risks are documented losses.
Real money. Real people.

160
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,500
None of this means DeFi is a mistake.
It means it requires understanding.

161
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:39,000
More understanding than most
financial products have ever demanded.

162
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,500
Don't interact with any protocol
you can't explain to someone else.

163
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:48,000
If you can't explain how it works,
you don't understand the risk.

164
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,500
That's not a warning against DeFi.
It's a standard for engaging with it.

165
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:57,000
Understand first. Then decide.

166
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:02,000
DeFi is powerful.
Smart contracts work.

167
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,500
Liquidity pools generate real fees.
The mechanism is real.

168
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:11,000
But it requires understanding
before trusting real value to it.

169
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,500
In Phase 4, we cover
security and self-custody.

170
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:20,000
How to hold crypto safely.
What a hardware wallet actually does.

171
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:24,500
And why "not your keys, not your coins"
isn't just a phrase.

172
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:29,000
It's a principle that has saved—
and cost—people millions.

173
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,500
See you in Phase 4.

174
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:38,000
Chain Clarity.
Blockchain, without the noise.

